Here's an example showing improper vs. proper use of Java string comparison in a web authentication scenario:

```java
// Login authentication handler
public class AuthenticationHandler {
    public boolean authenticateUser(String username, String password) {
        // Vulnerable: Using == for string comparison can lead to authentication bypass
        if (username == "admin" && password == "secretpass123") {
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    private void grantAccess(boolean isAuthenticated) {
        if (isAuthenticated) {
            System.out.println("Access granted");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Access denied");
        }
    }
}
```
***
```java
// Login authentication handler with proper string comparison
public class AuthenticationHandler {
    public boolean authenticateUser(String username, String password) {
        // Fixed: Using equals() for proper string content comparison
        if (username != null && password != null && 
            username.equals("admin") && password.equals("secretpass123")) {
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    private void grantAccess(boolean isAuthenticated) {
        if (isAuthenticated) {
            System.out.println("Access granted");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Access denied");
        }
    }
}
```
